    "q-109526": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109526",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "If ${X}_{1}∼N(12,9)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(9,3)$ are independent, then what is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ then \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2} & ∼N(12+9,9+3) \\\\  & ∼N(21,12).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $Y∼N(21,12).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
