    "q-109656": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109656",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "If ${X}_{1}∼N(7,16)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(-2,4)$ are independent, then what is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ then \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2} & ∼N(7-(-2),16+4) \\\\  & ∼N(9,20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $Y∼N(9,20).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
