    "q-109660": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109660",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "If ${X}_{1}∼N(11,2)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(-5,5)$ are independent, then what is the distribution of $Y=-2{X}_{1}+4{X}_{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y=-2{X}_{1}+4{X}_{2} & ∼N((-2)\\cdot 11+4\\cdot (-5),{2}^{2}\\cdot 2+(-4)^{2}\\cdot 5) \\\\  & ∼N(-42,88).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y∼N(-42,88).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
