    "q-109669": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109669",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "If ${X}_{1}∼N(7,1)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(6,3)$ are independent and $Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2},$ calculate $P(Y\\le 2.1).$ Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ | $\\,0.6443\\,$ | $\\,0.6480\\,$ | $\\,0.6517\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6736\\,$ | $\\,0.6772\\,$ | $\\,0.6808\\,$ | $\\,0.6844\\,$ | $\\,0.6879\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ | $\\,0.7190\\,$ | $\\,0.7224\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2})$, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2} & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(7-6,1+3) \\\\  & ∼N(1,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =1$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{4}=2.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(Y\\le 2.1).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le 2.1) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{2.1-1}{2}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.55) \\\\  & =Φ(0.55)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.55)≈0.7088.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le 2.1)≈0.71.\n$$",
