      "problem": "If ${X}_{1}∼N(8,{40}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(30,{30}^{2})$ are independent and $Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2},$ calculate $P(-31\\le Y\\le 31).$ Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th width=\"25%\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$z$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.09\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-0.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3557\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3520\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3483\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3936\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3897\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3859\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4325\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4286\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4247\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th width=\"25%\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$z$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.9\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8289\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8315\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8340\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,1.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8531\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8554\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8577\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,1.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8749\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8770\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8790\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n\n</tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2})$, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2} & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(8-30,{40}^{2}+{30}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(-22,2500).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =-22$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{2500}=50.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(-31\\le Y\\le 31).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-31\\le Y\\le 31) & =P(\\frac{-31-(-22)}{50}\\le Z\\le \\frac{31-(-22)}{50}) \\\\  & =P(-0.18\\le Z\\le 1.06) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.06)-P(Z<-0.18) \\\\  & =Φ(1.06)-Φ(-0.18)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.18) & ≈0.4286 \\\\ Φ(1.06) & ≈0.8554\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-31\\le Y\\le 31) & =Φ(1.06)-Φ(-0.18) \\\\  & ≈0.8554-0.4286 \\\\  & ≈0.43.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
