    "q-109668": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109668",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "If ${X}_{1}∼N(1,0.4)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(4,6)$ are independent and $Y=5{X}_{1}+{X}_{2},$ calculate $P(Y<13.4).$ Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ | $\\,0.8508\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8643\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ | $\\,0.8708\\,$ | $\\,0.8729\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8849\\,$ | $\\,0.8869\\,$ | $\\,0.8888\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ | $\\,0.8925\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & =5{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}∼N(5{\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2},{5}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(5\\cdot 1+4,25\\cdot 0.4+6) \\\\  & ∼N(9,16).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =9$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{16}=4.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(Y<13.4).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<13.4) & =P(Z<\\frac{13.4-9}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.1) \\\\  & =Φ(1.1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.1)≈0.8643.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y<13.4)≈0.86.\n$$",
