    "q-175476": {
      "dom_id": "step-q175476",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "A particular shop sells watermelons and melons. The weight of a watermelon is normally distributed with a mean of $10\\,\\text{kg}$ and a standard deviation of $4\\,\\text{kg}.$ The weight of a melon is normally distributed with a mean of $5\\,\\text{kg}$ and a standard deviation of $2\\,\\text{kg}.$ If one watermelon and one melon are randomly selected, what is the probability that the weight of the watermelon is more than $3$ times greater than the weight of the melon? You may assume that the weights are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ | $\\,0.7190\\,$ | $\\,0.7224\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ | $\\,0.7454\\,$ | $\\,0.7486\\,$ | $\\,0.7517\\,$ | $\\,0.7549\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ | $\\,0.7794\\,$ | $\\,0.7823\\,$ | $\\,0.7852\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Let $X$ denote the weight of a watermelon, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a melon. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(X>3Y)=P(X-3Y>0).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X∼N(10,{4}^{2})$ and $Y∼N(5,{2}^{2})$, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X-3Y & ∼N(10-3\\cdot 5,16+{3}^{2}\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ∼N(-5,52).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $X-3Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =-5$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{52}.$\n\nIn order to find $P(X-3Y>0),$ we convert $X-3Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X-3Y>0) & =P(Z>\\frac{0+5}{\\sqrt{52}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.69) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.69) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.69) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.69)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.69)≈0.7549.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X-3Y>0) & ≈1-Φ(0.69) \\\\  & ≈1-0.7549 \\\\  & ≈0.2451.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that the probability that the weight of a watermelon is more than $3$ times greater than the weight of a melon is $0.2451.$",
